\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 What are my options to present an ADU 3D model on a webpage to homeowner, knowing the following info, and using Ruby on Rails as the app to present it:\cb1 \
\
\cb3 3D Assets: Store the pre-designed, standardized .glTF 3D models of your modular ADUs in Active Storage, linked to their respective ADU database records.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \cb4 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 \strokec2 Your setup is already in a fantastic place. Using 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  files is the industry standard for web-based 3D, and leveraging Rails' Active Storage to serve them keeps your backend clean and organized.\
Since your backend heavy-lifting is mostly solved, your options depend entirely on how much 
\f3\b control
\f0\b0 , 
\f3\b customization
\f0\b0 , and 
\f3\b interactivity
\f0\b0  you want on the frontend. Here are the three best options for presenting these ADU models to homeowners.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 Option 1: 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 <model-viewer>
\f4\fs40 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (Highly Recommended)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 Developed by Google, 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 <model-viewer>
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  is a web component specifically designed to make embedding 3D models as easy as embedding an image or video. It is the absolute best starting point for 90% of use cases.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How it works:
\f0\b0  You include a single JavaScript library via CDN or your asset pipeline, and then you use a custom HTML tag 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 <model-viewer>
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  in your Rails view.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Pros:
\f0\b0  * Incredibly easy to implement.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Built-in support for augmented reality (AR) on iOS and Android (homeowners can drop the ADU into their actual backyard using their phone).\
\ls1\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Handles lighting, camera controls (orbit, zoom, pan), and responsiveness automatically.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Cons:
\f0\b0  Limited customization if you want to build a complex UI that heavily interacts with specific parts of the model (e.g., clicking a specific wall to change its texture).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 Rails Implementation Example:
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs30 \cf7 \cb3 \strokec7 Code snippet\cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs21 \cf7 \cb3 <script type="module" src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/model-viewer/3.1.1/model-viewer.min.js"></script>\
\
<model-viewer \
  src="<%= url_for(@adu.model_file) %>" \
  alt="3D model of <%= @adu.name %>" \
  camera-controls \
  auto-rotate \
  ar>\
</model-viewer>\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 Option 2: Three.js (The "Total Control" Route)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 Three.js is the underlying JavaScript library that powers most 3D on the web (including 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 <model-viewer>
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ). If you bypass the wrapper and use Three.js directly, you have ultimate control over the WebGL canvas.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How it works:
\f0\b0  You use the 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 GLTFLoader
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  module in Three.js to fetch the URL from your Rails backend, parse it, and add it to a custom 3D scene you build from scratch.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Pros:
\f0\b0  * Infinite customization.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Perfect if you want to build an "ADU Configurator" where homeowners can swap out siding materials, add modular decks, or change the time of day to see sun studies.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Cons:
\f0\b0  Steep learning curve. You have to manually code the camera controls, lighting setups, environment maps, and window resizing logic.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 Rails Implementation Tip:
\f0\b0  You would pass the Active Storage URL to your JavaScript controller (like a Stimulus controller) using a data attribute:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs30 \cf7 \cb3 \strokec7 Code snippet\cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs21 \cf7 \cb3 <div data-controller="three-viewer" \
     data-three-viewer-model-url-value="<%= url_for(@adu.model_file) %>">\
  </div>\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 Option 3: Babylon.js (The Architectural Engine)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 Babylon.js is Microsoft's answer to Three.js. It is a powerful, full-featured 3D engine that is heavily favored in the architectural and gaming spaces.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How it works:
\f0\b0  Similar to Three.js, you build the scene using JavaScript/TypeScript and load the 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  file via a URL.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Pros:
\f0\b0  Excellent out-of-the-box lighting for architectural scenes, incredibly robust documentation, and a highly active community for building visualization tools.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Cons:
\f0\b0  A large library size and a complex API. It's overkill if you just want the user to spin the model around.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

