\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Knowing all of the above, what workflow options do we have to create a collection of at-scale 3D models of ADUs that we can eventually add to a carousel of 3D models in a 3D model of a homeowner's backyard?\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \cb4 \strokec5 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 \strokec2 This is where the 2026 advancements in 3D web technology make your vision entirely possible\'97and frankly, it will look incredible.\
To achieve this, you are combining two fundamentally different types of 3D data: 
\f3\b standardized geometry meshes
\f0\b0  (your pre-designed ADUs) and 
\f3\b photorealistic environmental data
\f0\b0  (the homeowner's backyard).\
Here is the exact, step-by-step workflow to build this out using Rails.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 Phase 1: The ADU Pipeline (Standardized Meshes)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 You already know you are using 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  files for your ADUs, but to make sure they drop perfectly into a real-world backyard, you need strict rule enforcement during export.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The 1-to-1 Rule:
\f0\b0  glTF natively uses meters as its unit of measurement. Ensure your CAD/BIM software (Revit, Chief Architect, SketchUp) exports the ADUs so that 
\f3\b 1 unit = 1 meter
\f0\b0 . If you don't standardize this, your ADU might look like a dollhouse or Godzilla when dropped into the backyard.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Origin Point:
\f0\b0  Set the pivot/origin point of every ADU model to the bottom-center of the foundation. This ensures that when the code places the model at an elevation of 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 0
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , it sits perfectly flush on the grass.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Storage:
\f0\b0  Keep doing what you are doing. Store these as 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glb
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (binary glTF) files in Rails Active Storage, linked to an 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 AduModel
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  database record.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 Phase 2: The Backyard Pipeline (Gaussian Splatting)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 Traditional photogrammetry (turning photos into a 3D mesh) struggles terribly with things like trees, chainlink fences, and grass. To get a photorealistic backyard, you will use 
\f3\b 3D Gaussian Splatting
\f0\b0 .  \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f6\fs30 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 Medium\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa340\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 \strokec2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Capture:
\f0\b0  You can either have the homeowner use a free consumer app (like Scaniverse, Polycam, or Luma AI) on their smartphone, or you can send a professional with a drone. They simply walk around the yard capturing video/photos.  \uc0\u8232 
\f6\fs30 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 Medium\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \strokec2 \
\ls9\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Cleanup:
\f0\b0  Raw captures often include unnecessary data (like the neighbor's house or floating artifacts). You can run the capture through a browser-based cleanup tool like 
\f3\b SuperSplat
\f0\b0  to crop and refine the yard.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Export:
\f0\b0  Thanks to the 2026 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 KHR_gaussian_splatting
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  standard, you can export this backyard as a 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  file (or a highly compressed 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .spz
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  file for faster web streaming).\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Storage:
\f0\b0  Upload this backyard file to Active Storage, linked to the 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 Homeowner
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  or 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 Project
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  record.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 Phase 3: The Web Presentation (The Hybrid Viewer)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 Because you are now mixing Gaussian Splats (the yard) with standard meshes (the ADU) and requiring a custom UI (the carousel), the basic 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 <model-viewer>
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  tag won't cut it. You need a dedicated web engine.\
Here are your two best workflow options for the frontend:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 Option A: Three.js + A Splatting Library (Best for React/Stimulus Ecosystems)
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How it works:
\f0\b0  You use vanilla 
\f3\b Three.js
\f0\b0  to handle the standard ADU 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  models, and you bolt on a modern open-source library like 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 mkkellogg/GaussianSplats3D
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  or 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 Spark
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  to render the backyard.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Flow:
\f0\b0  The scene loads the backyard splat first. Your HTML/CSS carousel (managed by a Rails Stimulus controller) sits over the canvas. When a user clicks an ADU, Stimulus tells Three.js to fetch that specific ADU's Active Storage URL and drop the mesh into the center of the scene.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 Option B: Babylon.js (The Native Powerhouse)
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How it works:
\f0\b0  
\f3\b Babylon.js
\f0\b0  has built-in, native support for mixing 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 GaussianSplattingMesh
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  and standard glTF models right out of the box without needing third-party plugins.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Flow:
\f0\b0  It works identically to the Three.js route, but Babylon handles GPU-picking a bit more gracefully. This is useful if you want to allow the homeowner to click and drag the ADU around their yard (locking the movement to the ground plane so it doesn't float into the sky).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf2 The User Experience\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34 \cf2 When the homeowner logs into your Rails app:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The browser downloads their compressed backyard 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  and the default ADU 
\f2\fs22\fsmilli11250 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 .glTF
\f0\fs34 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  from Active Storage.\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Because both files adhere strictly to real-world scale (1 unit = 1 meter), the ADU immediately looks perfectly sized next to their actual patio furniture and trees.\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 They click through the HTML carousel, instantly swapping the ADU models in the center of their yard.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
